Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Anterior knee ache, Exacerbation descending stairs, Positive movie theater sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-042
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of anterior knee pain rendering the patient unable to navigate the stairs in their home following a long car journey.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who developed severe, sharp anterior knee pain and a sudden giving-way sensation after sitting for a prolonged period, leaving them stranded on the ground floor and unable to climb the stairs to their bedroom. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate severe patellofemoral tracking dysfunction from an acute patellar subluxation or meniscal tear, and to provide immediate mechanical taping and analgesia to restore basic, safe home mobility before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My kneecap is grinding and burning; after sitting in the car for three hours, I went to walk up my front steps and my knee just collapsed in agony."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute flare of bilateral (worse on right) anterior knee pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of a diffuse, dull ache behind the kneecap over the past 2 months; classically worsened by descending stairs or deep squatting. Today, the patient experienced a severe "movie theater sign"—an intense ache and stiffness after sitting with the knees flexed during a 3-hour drive. Upon attempting to ascend the stairs at home, the patient felt a sharp catching, grinding sensation behind the patella and severe pain, resulting in an inability to continue.
Functional Status: Ambulating cautiously on flat ground; completely unable to negotiate stairs due to severe mechanical pain and fear of the knee buckling.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient's bedroom and bathroom are on the second floor; currently stranded.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated, resting with the right leg completely straight.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals relative weakness/atrophy of the vastus medialis obliquus (VMO) muscle compared to the bulky lateral quadriceps. Mild, intermittent peripatellar fullness noted, but crucially, there is no tense, large intra-articular effusion. Exquisite pain is provoked by direct downward compression of the patella into the trochlear groove (patellar grind test). Observable lateral tracking of the patella during dynamic active extension of the knee from a seated position. Significant tightness of the iliotibial band (positive Ober's test) and hamstrings identified. Ligamentous testing (ACL, PCL, MCL, LCL) is stable. Joint line tenderness is absent (negative for meniscal tear).
Environmental Observations: A steep flight of stairs separates the patient from the sleeping quarters.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Ibuprofen 400mg 1 hour ago with minimal relief of the mechanical catching pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome (PFPS) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Patellar Dislocation/Subluxation - Ruled out by the absence of a distinct traumatic "popping out" event, no medial retinacular tearing/tenderness, and the patella remaining within the groove (albeit tracking poorly).

Patellar Tendinopathy (Jumper's Knee) - Ruled out by maximal tenderness being located deep/retropatellar rather than strictly at the inferior pole of the patella at the tendon origin.

Chondromalacia Patellae - A chronic structural manifestation of this syndrome, but the acute pain crisis is driven by the dynamic tracking dysfunction and resulting acute synovial irritation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, functionally limiting exacerbation of patellofemoral pain, classically triggered by prolonged flexion (movie theater sign) and stairs. The critical clinical step was differentiating this severe mechanical tracking pain from an acute structural failure (subluxation or ligament tear). Physical examination clearly localized the pathology to the patellofemoral articulation, confirming lateral maltracking and VMO weakness without joint instability. The sudden inability to navigate stairs rendered the patient functionally stranded. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient sleeping on the floor with unmanaged pain. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the biomechanical nature of the pain, and allowed for the immediate application of specialized McConnell taping to mechanically realign the patella, instantly restoring the ability to climb stairs safely.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 5 days. Applied firm McConnell taping (rigid strapping tape) to physically glide the patella medially and correct the lateral maltracking.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Ascend the stairs leading with the "good" left leg, and descend leading with the "bad" right leg, keeping the right leg as straight as possible. Consequence: Minimization of the compressive forces acting on the kneecap during the deepest part of the knee bend, completely avoiding the sharp, grinding pain spikes (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform straight-leg raises while lying in bed, consciously squeezing the inner thigh muscle (VMO) before lifting. Consequence: Targeted waking up of the lazy inner muscle, which actively pulls the kneecap back into its proper central groove (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory referral to a physiotherapist for VMO strengthening and biomechanical correction.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided rigid strapping tape and instructed the patient on re-application.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to monitor progress with physical therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the knee suddenly locks completely and cannot be straightened, if the kneecap visibly dislocates to the outside of the leg, or if the knee rapidly balloons with fluid.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of joint mechanics. X-rays are usually normal but can show how the kneecap sits in its groove. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, burning pain will subside with the taping and anti-inflammatories over 3-7 days. However, the condition will absolutely return if the muscle imbalance between the inner and outer thigh is not corrected with physiotherapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your kneecap physically pops out of place, or if the knee locks entirely and you cannot straighten it no matter what you do.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your kneecap is supposed to glide smoothly in a groove, but your outer thigh muscles are too tight and your inner thigh muscles are too weak. This is pulling the kneecap off-center, causing it to grind against the bone underneath, especially after sitting or using stairs. The rigid tape physically pulls the kneecap back into the center of the groove, stopping the grinding instantly.
Actionable Steps: Leave the tape on for the next 48 hours. When you use the stairs tonight, step up with your good leg first, and step down with your bad leg first. This simple trick takes the massive pressure off the damaged cartilage.

